  "background": "The letter presents Paul as imprisoned, nearing the end of his ministry, and writing to Timothy as a beloved younger coworker. If read as a direct Pauline letter, it is commonly placed late in Paul’s life, often around A.D. 64–67, after the period narrated in Acts. Modern scholarship debates the authorship and dating of the Pastoral Epistles, including 2 Timothy, while traditional interpretation receives the letter as Paul’s final personal correspondence. The study should distinguish what the letter explicitly says from later historical reconstruction while allowing the authorship discussion to remain secondary to the text’s pastoral message.\n\nSecond Timothy does not celebrate isolated heroism. Paul asks Timothy to remember family influence, receive grace, cultivate his gift, depend on the Spirit, entrust truth to reliable people, avoid destructive arguments, pursue holiness with others, recognize manipulative religion, continue in Scripture, preach patiently, and come quickly because Paul needs companionship. Statements about suffering, the “last days,” persecution, Scripture, discipline, and finishing the race must be read in context. The letter calls for durable faithfulness without requiring people to remain in abuse, ignore mental or physical limits, weaponize the Bible, or treat disagreement as proof of apostasy.",
