      "opening": "Read 2 Timothy 1:1–18 and mark promise of life, beloved child, ancestors, prayers, tears, sincere faith, Lois, Eunice, gift, fan into flame, laying on of hands, fear, power, love, self-control, ashamed, testimony, prisoner, suffering, power of God, saved, called, grace, Christ Jesus, abolished death, life, immortality, gospel, suffer, ashamed, entrusted, pattern, sound words, Holy Spirit, guard, turned away, Onesiphorus, refreshed, chains, searched, found, service. Ask what Paul gives Timothy before telling him what to do.",
      "context": "The letter presents Paul as imprisoned and writing to Timothy with deep affection. Timothy’s sincere faith has been shaped by the witness of his grandmother Lois and mother Eunice, but Paul addresses Timothy as someone who must personally cultivate what he has received. The gift associated with Paul’s laying on of hands is not fully identified here, so readers should not pretend to know more than the text says. The emphasis is that entrusted grace requires active stewardship.\n\nThe chapter moves between courage and relationship. Timothy is told not to be ashamed of Christ’s testimony or of Paul’s imprisonment, yet Paul also names the pain of abandonment and the refreshment brought by Onesiphorus. “Share in suffering” is tied to the gospel and God’s power; it is not a command to seek suffering or remain in preventable danger. The Spirit’s power, love, and self-control oppose cowardly withdrawal, but verse 7 should not be used to shame anxiety, trauma, depression, or other health conditions.",
